=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported fault — SRV1 having "crashed" or "fallen off the network" causing
DNS resolution failures for jeremysitlab.com — does not exist. All components
are fully healthy.

SRV1 (10.5.0.4) is online with its interface up and correctly addressed. The
BIND authoritative name server (named, PID 30) is running and actively bound
to UDP port 53 on the 10.5.0.4 address. The zone file db.jeremysitlab is
correctly populated with A records for srv1.jeremysitlab.com (→ 10.5.0.4) and
a wildcard record covering all other campus hostnames under jeremysitlab.com.

DNS queries issued from Office A endpoints (pc2 at 10.1.0.100, laptop1 at
10.6.0.100, pc1 at 10.1.0.100) and the Office B endpoint (pc3) all resolve
srv1.jeremysitlab.com to 10.5.0.4 successfully via SRV1. ICMP ping from pc2
to SRV1 shows 0% packet loss across four tests, and a traceroute confirms an
intact four-hop routed path (pc2 → dswa gateway → core switch → dswb gateway
→ srv1). The "Got recursion not available" warning visible to clients is
intentional: named.conf explicitly sets recursion no, making SRV1
authoritative-only, which is correct and by design.

Root cause: There is no fault. The ticket is based on a false premise — the
user assumed DNS-name failures from their application layer implied SRV1 was
down, but live lab probes confirm the server is up, the DNS daemon is
responding correctly, and all client-to-server paths are reachable.

Corrective action: No infrastructure change is required. Close the ticket and
advise the user to re-test; if their local application still reports failures,
check local client-side DNS cache or application configuration rather than the
server infrastructure.
=== END ===
